{"id":"kth-smallest-in-a-search-tree","lc":"kth-smallest-element-in-a-bst","title":"K-th Smallest in a Search Tree","diff":"medium","pattern":"trees","noted":true,"tags":["bst","inorder","dfs"],"statement":"<p>You get the <code>root</code> of a binary search tree with distinct values: everything in a node's left subtree is smaller than it, and everything in its right subtree is larger. You also get an integer <code>k</code>.</p>\n<p>Return the <code>k</code>-th smallest value in the tree. <code>k</code> <b>counts from 1</b>: <code>k = 1</code> asks for the minimum, <code>k = n</code> for the maximum.</p>\n<p>Trees are written in level order with <code>null</code> for a missing child.</p>","examples":[{"in":"root = [40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 50, 70, null, 15], k = 3","out":"20","why":"In sorted order the values are 10, 15, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70. The third is 20. The third value in level order would be 60, and a 0-based count gives 30."},{"in":"root = [40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 50, 70, null, 15], k = 2","out":"15","why":"15 is the right child of the minimum 10, so it comes right after 10 even though it sits on the deepest level."},{"in":"root = [5, 3], k = 2","out":"5","why":"With k equal to the number of nodes, the answer is the largest value, here the root."}],"constraints":["1 ≤ k ≤ number of nodes ≤ 100 000","-10⁹ ≤ Node.val ≤ 10⁹, all distinct","The tree is at most 500 levels deep"],"fn":{"name":"kthSmallest","params":[["root","TreeNode"],["k","int"]],"returns":"int"},"tests":[{"args":[[40,20,60,10,30,50,70,null,15],3],"expected":20},{"args":[[40,20,60,10,30,50,70,null,15],2],"expected":15},{"args":[[5,3],2],"expected":5},{"args":[[40,20,60,10,30,50,70,null,15],1],"expected":10},{"args":[[40,20,60,10,30,50,70,null,15],5],"expected":40},{"args":[[40,20,60,10,30,50,70,null,15],8],"expected":70},{"args":[[7],1],"expected":7},{"args":[[5,3],1],"expected":3},{"args":[[5,null,8],1],"expected":5},{"args":[[-4,-9,6,null,-7,0],2],"expected":-7},{"args":[[-4,-9,6,null,-7,0],4],"expec