q = [t]; for (let i = 0; i < q.length; i++) { const x = q[i]; if (x) { o.push(x.v); q.push(x.L, x.R); } else o.push(null); } while (o.length && o[o.length - 1] === null) o.pop(); return o; };\n  const bst = vals => { let t = null; for (const v of vals) { const x = nd(v); if (!t) { t = x; continue; } let c = t; for (;;) { const k = v < c.v ? \"L\" : \"R\"; if (!c[k]) { c[k] = x; break; } c = c[k]; } } return t; };\n  // random insertion order keeps the expected height near 4.3 ln n, far below 500\n  const vals = big ? r.distinct(100000, -1000000000, 1000000000) : r.distinct(r.int(1, 10), -20, 20);\n  if (!big && r.bool(0.2)) vals.sort((a, b) => r.bool() ? a - b : b - a); // sometimes a one-sided chain\n  return [ser(bst(vals)), big ? r.int(90000, 100000) : r.int(1, vals.length)];\n}","hints":["Which traversal of a search tree visits the values in sorted order?","An in-order walk (left subtree, node, right subtree) produces the values from smallest to largest. The answer is the k-th value it produces.","You do not need the whole sorted list. Walk in order with an explicit stack, count each node as you pop it, and stop when the count reaches k."],"explain":{"intuition":"<p>In a search tree, everything smaller than a node is on its left and everything larger is on its right. So \"visit the left subtree, then the node, then the right subtree\" emits the values in increasing order. The k-th value emitted is the answer.</p>\n<p>The simple version collects the whole in-order list and indexes it. The better version stops early: walk in order with a stack, count nodes as they are emitted, and return the moment the count hits k. Then a small k costs only the path down to the minimum plus k steps, like a paginated list that fetches only what the current page needs.</p>","steps":["Start with an empty stack and <code>node = root</code>.","Push <code>node</code> and move to <code>node.left</code> until it is null: the stack now leads down to the smallest unvisited value.","Pop a node. This is the next value in sorted order; decrease <code>k</code>.","If <code>k</code> reached 0, return that node's value.","Otherwise set <code>node</code> to its right child and repeat from step 2."],"pitfalls":["Treating k as 0-based. The first example returns 30 instead of 20 that way.","Using level order or pre-order. Only in-order follows the sorted order of a search tree.","Going only down the left spine. The second smallest can be the minimum's right child, like 15 in the second example."],"complexity":{"time":"O(h + k)","space":"O(h)"}},"solutions":[{"title":"Collect the in-order list","idea":"In-order traversal lists the values in sorted order. Collect them all and take index k - 1.","time":"O(n)","space":"O(n)","ts":"function kthSmallest(root: TreeNode | null, k: number): number {\n  const sorted: number[] = [];\n  const walk = (n: TreeNode | null): void => {\n    if (!n) return;\n    walk(n.left);\n    sorted.push(n.val);\n    walk(n.right);\n  };\n  walk(root);\n  return sorted[k - 1];\n}","py":"class Solution:\n    def kthSmallest(self, root: Optional[TreeNode], k: int) -> int:\n        ordered = []\n\n        def walk(n):\n            if n:\n                walk(n.left)\n                ordered.append(n.val)\n                walk(n.right)\n\n        walk(root)\n        return ordered[k - 1]","js":"function kthSmallest(root, k) {\n  const sorted = [];\n  const walk = (n) => {\n    if (!n) return;\n    walk(n.left);\n    sorted.push(n.val);\n    walk(n.right);\n  };\n  walk(root);\n  return sorted[k - 1];\n}"},{"title":"In-order with a stack, stop at k","idea":"Walk in order with an explicit stack and count the nodes as they come out. Stop as soon as the k-th one appears.","time":"O(h + k)","space":"O(h)","ts":"function kthSmallest(root: TreeNode | null, k: number): number {\n  const stack: TreeNode[] = [];\n  let n = root;\n  for (;;) {\n    while (n) { stack.push(n); n = n.left; } // slide down to the smallest unvisited value\n    n = stack.pop()!;\n    if (--k === 0) return n.val;\n    n = n.right;\n  }\n}","py":"class Solution:\n    def kthSmallest(self, root: Optional[TreeNode], k: int) -> int:\n        stack, n = [], root\n        while True:\n            while n:  # slide down to the smallest unvisited value\n                stack.append(n)\n                n = n.left\n            n = stack.pop()\n            k -= 1\n            if k == 0:\n                return n.val\n            n = n.right","js":"function kthSmallest(root, k) {\n  const stack = [];\n  let n = root;\n  for (; ; ) {\n    while (n) {\n      stack.push(n);\n      n = n.left;\n    }\n    n = stack.pop();\n    if (--k === 0) return n.val;\n    n = n.right;\n  }\n}"}],"followups":["The tree changes often and you are asked for the k-th smallest many times. What extra number per node makes each query O(h)?","Return the k-th largest instead. What is the smallest change to the stack walk?"],"related":[],"kind":"function","nEx":3,"nBig":2,"lcTitle":"Kth Smallest Element in a BST","acc":0.007743578904864954,"co":[["Uber",84,180],["Expedia",54,0],["Bloomberg",50,90],["tcs",50,90],["Oracle",49,0],["Amazon",44,180],["Microsoft",43,180],["Google",41,180],["Meta",35,0],["TikTok",26,0]]}